  "raw_text": "{\"options\": [\"For George, the dream gives him a reason to keep working hard even though his life is lonely, but for Lennie, it is mostly about getting to take care of rabbits, showing how their unique form of companionship provides a sense of purpose.\", \"Most of the other workers on the ranch have no one, so they are surprised that the two men travel together, yet their friendship offers a rare and valuable companionship that contrasts with the isolation of the others.\", \"Steinbeck uses their friendship to show how important it is to have someone who cares about you, highlighting the vital role companionship plays in the lives of two men who otherwise might have been alone.\", \"When the dream falls apart at the end, it shows how hard it was for poor workers to change their lives during the Great Depression, even though the companionship they shared was their only hope.\", \"The companionship between George and Lennie serves as a powerful example of how having someone who cares about you can sustain a dream of owning a small farm together.\"]}\n",
